a. Bootstrap of Returns

    Generate many synthetic price paths by shuffling the historical returns (with or without block bootstrap to preserve autocorrelation).

    Run your discovered model on each synthetic path.

    Compare the actual OOS Sharpe ratio to the distribution from synthetic paths. If many synthetic paths produce similar or better performance, the edge may be illusory.

b. Surrogate Data with Preserved Properties

    Use algorithms that generate surrogate time series with the same mean, variance, autocorrelation, and even higher‑order properties (e.g., IAAFT surrogates).

    If the model performs well on surrogates, it might be exploiting subtle statistical features that are also present in the surrogates—possibly still spurious, but you can then test if those features are economically meaningful.

c. Regime Shuffling

    Randomly permute the order of market regimes (e.g., high/low volatility periods) to see if the model’s success depends on a specific sequence of regimes.

7. Economic Plausibility

Finally, ask: Does the discovered hypothesis make economic sense? If a model finds that buying when the moon is full yields high returns, it’s probably random. But if it finds that low volatility followed by a volatility spike predicts mean reversion, that could be rooted in market microstructure or behavioral finance. Even after quantitative validation, qualitative reasoning adds a layer of protection.
8. Document and Iterate

Keep a detailed log of:

    The search space and discovery algorithm.

    All models considered (including failures).

    Performance in IS, validation, OOS, and synthetic tests.

    Any decisions made during the process.

If a model passes all these hurdles, it may be ready for forward testing (paper trading). Remember that even robust models can fail when market structure changes, so ongoing monitoring is essential.
